{
  "id": "frontal",
  "name": "Frontal session (Feelgood one-phase / lucid-dream step 2)",
  "category": "courses",
  "evidence": "claim",
  "origin": "Todd Murphy (8 Coil Shakti one-phase Feelgood session and lucid-dream series); signal templates licensed from Persinger & Koren",
  "uT": 1,
  "summary": "Murphy's frontal session: the amygdala and hippocampal signals one after the other, with 4-s pauses, over both frontal lobes for 30 min. Murphy calls the one-phase frontal session his most effective Feelgood (mood) session, and uses it as the sixth session of his lucid-dream series, after five temporal sessions (meditation-prep).",
  "notes": "Four coils over the frontal lobes, two over each eye (Fp1, AF3 and Fp2, AF4 on CH5-8, so the same 8-coil set-up runs meditation-prep on CH1-4); the vendor shows only photos, so the site names are an inference. The signals are proprietary. One pass here is: the amygdala signal, approximated by the built-in accelerating burst (6 to 30 Hz, 230 points of 3 ms = 0.69 s; the 230-point length is from Ryczko & Persinger 2002); 4 s of silence (the vendor: the two signals are \"generated one after the other ... with a four-second latency\"); the hippocampal 'LTP' signal, approximated by four theta bursts of four 100 Hz pulses (Larson & Lynch timing) repeated at 5.4 Hz, the spectral peak the vendor reports for it (0.6 s, with 1-ms points instead of the vendor's 3-ms points so the 100 Hz pulses fit); then 4 s of silence again. That gives about 8 s of field per minute (the vendor states 9.6 s). The vendor gives 20 or 30 min for the one-phase Feelgood session (stop at 20 min for the shorter one) and 30 min for the frontal session of the lucid-dream series. Murphy says \"you should begin with the one-phase feelgood session\"; his two-phase Feelgood plays the same signal over the temporal lobes first and then the frontal lobes in one session. For lucid dreams he describes five weekly temporal sessions and then one frontal session, calls the temporal sessions \"probably ... a prerequisite\", and reports cases of lucid dreams \"that very night\"; such reports are few. The vendor states that mood effects often show the next morning and last 3-4 days, and that the session works \"like aspirin\", with little effect if you already feel good. The design rests on small single-lab Persinger studies of weekly burst-firing fields and mood (Baker-Price & Persinger 2003, temporal or left-frontal placement). An independent randomized sham-controlled test of the 8 Coil Shakti amygdala signal over the temporal lobes found no effect on emotional responses (Gendle & McGrath 2012; Murphy argues it used the wrong session), and a double-blind God Helmet test found no field effect (Granqvist 2005). Not a treatment, and not for people with a psychiatric diagnosis or medication, epilepsy or migraine. You may feel \"a bit foggy\" right after; too many frontal or temporal series in a row can bring \"malaise\", so alternate them.",
  "setting": "Evening, seated or reclined; no darkness or silence needed. For dream work, end the session 1-1.5 h before sleep.",
  "schedule": "Mood: six sessions, weekly or every 72 h (never more often), then a 3-week break; if a series is interrupted, wait 3 days and restart. Lucid dreams: five weekly temporal sessions (meditation-prep), then this one on the target night.",
  "targets": ["vmpfc", "left-dlpfc", "right-dlpfc"],
